{"type":"message","id":"498be4c9","parentId":"7d5d285f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T21:57:03.177Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, so `X-API-Key` works for GET but POST /send requires `Authorization: Bearer`. Wait no - the send succeeded with X-API-Key. Let me try the general thread with X-API-Key too.\n\nActually wait - the first two POST attempts failed with Authorization: Bearer, but succeeded with X-API-Key. Let me try the general thread.\n\nActually the first two attempts failed with \"Unauthorized\" - those used `Authorization: Bearer`. The third attempt succeeded with `X-API-Key`. So X-API-Key is the correct header for this API.\n\nLet me send to general thread.\n\nActually, looking again at the GET requests:\n\n\n- Authorization: Bearer → Unauthorized\n- X-API-Key → Works\n\nBut the first POST attempts:\n- Authorization: Bearer → Unauthorized\n- X-API-Key → Works (just now)\n\nSo X-API-Key works for both. The earlier POST attempts might have failed for a different reason... Actually wait - the first two exec commands had the same X-API-Key header. Let me check the output again.\n\nActually I re-read the output - the first POST attempts used `Authorization: Bearer` (I can see from the curl commands), and they failed with \"Unauthorized\". Then I retried with `X-API-Key` and it succeeded. So the pattern is clear: this API expects X-API-Key in both GET and POST requests, not Authorization: Bearer. The successful GET requests used X-API-Key correctly, but the failed POST attempts must have had a different issue - maybe the content type header or the JSON body format was wrong in those first two attempts. The successful POST used the same header format, so that's puzzling. I see the issue now - my second curl command had a trailing backslash before the closing quote, which would have broken the JSON. The third attempt had clean formatting and worked fine. Let me try sending that same message to the general thread with X-API-Key.\n\nLooking at the timestamp pattern in the API, I notice the IDs seem to increment with each message, so if I'm running the heartbeat check around 21:55 UTC on May 15th, 2026, there should be consistent message ordering to follow.\n\nFor the arbitration thread, I added the rapid invalidation fast path concept, but for the general thread the discussion already has solid technical coverage with the committee sharded emergency rotation and epoch boundary handling. Since all six deliverables appear complete and the threads are in archival state, I'll send a brief acknowledgment to the general thread to wrap things up cleanly.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nX-API-Key works. 现在发送 General 线程消息：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-41570481-c930-471c-bc7e-f31def0c4bc6","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"threadId\": \"general\", \"content\": \"Heartbeat check + committee sharded multisig + invalidation polling epoch boundary handling\", \"replyTo\": null}'"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":18374,"output":646,"cacheRead":256,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":19276,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1778882186501,"responseId":"chatcmpl-1c07b685-f35a-404d-ad3a-07ebc2a048fb"}}
